## Cockpit communication protocol

When a tmux cockpit is running, treat it as managed state. Use
`cockpit-queue` plus `cockpit-overseer tick` for normal product missions.
Use `cockpit-protocol` for receipts, lifecycle, durable dialogs/ACKs, `tail`,
`watch`, `meta cockpit --json`, and `status --workers all --json`.
`dispatch --bootstrap` only primes roles during setup; `send` / `nudge` are
raw-input diagnostic or explicitly human-requested exceptions, never normal
mission, approval or cancel operations.

Use `cockpit-overseer status` / `cockpit-overseer loop` for compact status
checks and short-loop polling.

Follow global `e2e-cockpit` and the toolkit README operator walkthrough.
Dispatch is `pending-dispatch` sequence 0 without heartbeat. Workers run the
exact `accept-dispatch` receipt and start only on `accepted` / `start_work=true`
(sequence 1); duplicates never restart work. `heartbeat` running starts at 2.
`ask` / `access-prompt` report explicit prompts; `pending` / `read-question`
return durable mission-status JSON. Relay only explicit human replies;
`hold --answers` does not approve. Inspect `status.pending_commands` and
`command-status`; accepted-worker cancel/replace needs accepted then applied ACK
with matching digest and typed `--result`. Reuse IDs/digests; private bodies stay
outside the journal, with no full secrets in metadata or pane archives.
Durable lifecycle is separate from pane diagnosis (`available`, `working`,
`awaiting-approval`, `held`, `blocked`, `unreachable`). Uninstrumented prompts
need explicit reporting, never inferred answers or automatic permission approval.

Expired or legacy missing-deadline unaccepted dispatches stay reserved until
operator inspection proves no executing worker. Follow global `recover-dispatch`
guidance (`--dispatch-command`, recovery `--command-id`, `--worker`,
`--inspect-safe`, `--by operator`, `--reason`, typed `--evidence`); it fences late
receipts, not kills workers or forges ACKs. Accepted work must stop cooperatively.
Wakes invoke ticks with stored root/session/window and all five required intent
fields; follow the global scheduler example. `--dry-run` is read-only,
`stop` aliases `cancel` (including fired recurring); `migrate` diagnoses only.
Stop legacy jobs explicitly, bootstrap/bind/preflight, then reschedule.

Do not inspect or control the cockpit with ad-hoc raw `tmux` commands. This
applies to read-only status requests as well as worker coordination. Do not
improvise `tmux ls`, `tmux list-windows`, `tmux list-panes`,
`tmux capture-pane`, `tmux load-buffer`, `tmux paste-buffer`, or
`tmux send-keys` unless the user explicitly asks for raw tmux diagnostics.

If the parent repo has `graphify-out/graph.json` and `graphify` is available,
use `graphify query "<question>" --graph "$REPO/graphify-out/graph.json"`
before broad text search for codebase, architecture, file-relationship, and
project-content questions. Pass the graph path in worker briefs when it helps
orientation.

